const KB = [{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Introduction to Economics","x":"Economics is the study of how people, businesses, and governments use limited resources to satisfy unlimited wants.\nExample: You have $10 but want food, a phone card, and a book. You cannot buy everything, so you must decide how to use the $10.\nKey idea: Economics is mainly about scarcity, choice, and how resources are used."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Human Wants, Scarcity and Choice","x":"Wants are things people desire.\nExamples:\n• Food\n• Clothes\n• A phone\n• A car\n• Education\nScarcity means resources are limited while human wants are unlimited.\nChoice means selecting one thing from several alternatives.\nExample: If you have $5 and can buy either lunch or a notebook, you have to choose."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Opportunity Cost","x":"Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative that you give up when making a choice.\nExample: You have $20. You choose to buy a textbook instead of buying a football jersey.\nYour opportunity cost is the football jersey you gave up.\nSimple formula:\nChoice → What you choose\nOpportunity cost → What you give up"},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Scale of Preference","x":"A scale of preference is a list of wants arranged from the most important to the least important.\nExample:\n1. Food\n2. School materials\n3. Transportation\n4. New shoes\n5. Entertainment\nBecause resources are limited, the person starts with the most important need."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Factors of Production","x":"Factors of production are the resources used to produce goods and services.\nThere are four main factors:\nExample: A bakery needs land, workers, machines, and someone to organize the business.\nFactor | Meaning | Reward\nLand | Natural resources | Rent\nLabour | Human effort | Wages\nCapital | Man-made resources used in production | Interest\nEntrepreneur | Person who organizes production | Profit"},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Production","x":"Production means creating goods and services or adding value to them.\nExamples:\n• A farmer produces rice.\n• A teacher provides education.\n• A carpenter produces furniture.\nThere are three broad types:\n• Primary production: agriculture, fishing, mining\n• Secondary production: manufacturing and construction\n• Tertiary production: services such as banking, teaching and transportation"},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Division of Labour and Specialization","x":"Division of labour means breaking a production process into different tasks performed by different workers.\nSpecialization means concentrating on a particular job or activity.\nExample: In a shoe factory, one worker may cut leather, another may sew, and another may package the shoes.\nBenefit: Production can become faster and more efficient."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Economic Systems","x":"An economic system is the way a society organizes the production and distribution of goods and services.\nMain systems include:\n• Capitalism: individuals and private businesses own most resources.\n• Socialism: the government has greater control over resources and production.\n• Mixed economy: both private businesses and government participate."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Demand","x":"Demand is the quantity of a good or service that consumers are willing and able to buy at a given price and time.\nGenerally:\nPrice ↑ → Quantity demanded ↓\nPrice ↓ → Quantity demanded ↑\nThis is called the law of demand.\nExample: If the price of a phone falls, more people may be willing to buy it."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Supply","x":"Supply is the quantity of a good or service that producers are willing and able to offer for sale at a given price and time.\nGenerally:\nPrice ↑ → Quantity supplied ↑\nPrice ↓ → Quantity supplied ↓\nThis is the law of supply."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Price Determination","x":"The price of many goods is influenced by the interaction between demand and supply.\nWhere demand and supply meet, we get equilibrium.\nEquilibrium price is the price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nIf demand is greater than supply, there may be a shortage.\nIf supply is greater than demand, there may be a surplus."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Markets","x":"A market is a place or system where buyers and sellers exchange goods and services.\nExamples:\n• Physical markets\n• Online markets\n• Labour markets\n• Financial markets\nMarkets can have different levels of competition."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Market Structures","x":"Two important examples are:\nPerfect competition: Many buyers and sellers compete, and no single seller controls the market.\nMonopoly: One major seller controls the supply of a particular good or service.\nEach market structure can affect prices, competition, and consumer choice."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Money","x":"Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment for goods and services.\nThe main functions of money are:\n1. Medium of exchange\n2. Measure of value\n3. Store of value\n4. Standard of deferred payment\nBefore modern money, people often used barter, exchanging one good for another."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Banking","x":"A bank is a financial institution that accepts deposits, provides loans, and offers other financial services.\nCommercial banks serve individuals and businesses.\nA central bank manages important aspects of a country's monetary and financial system.\nBanks help the economy by:\n• Keeping money safely\n• Providing loans\n• Facilitating payments\n• Supporting investment"},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Inflation","x":"Inflation is a sustained increase in the general level of prices of goods and services.\nExample: If the general prices of food, transportation and other necessities keep rising, the purchasing power of money falls.\nPossible causes include:\n• Increased demand\n• Rising production costs\n• Excessive growth in money supply"},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"National Income","x":"National income refers to the income generated by an economy over a particular period.\nImportant terms include:\nGDP: The value of final goods and services produced within a country's borders.\nGNP: The value of goods and services produced by a country's citizens and businesses, including relevant income from abroad.\nPer capita income: Average income per person."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Population and Labour","x":"Population is the total number of people living in a particular area.\nThe labour force consists broadly of people who are available and willing to work.\nEmployment means having productive work.\nUnemployment occurs when people who are willing and able to work cannot find suitable work.\nHigh unemployment can reduce household income and economic output."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Agriculture and the Economy","x":"Agriculture is very important to many African economies, including Liberia.\nIt provides:\n• Food\n• Employment\n• Raw materials\n• Income\n• Export opportunities\nExamples include rice farming, cocoa, rubber, oil palm and fishing.\nImproving agriculture can contribute to economic growth and development."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"International Trade","x":"International trade is the exchange of goods and services between countries.\nImports: Goods and services bought from other countries.\nExports: Goods and services sold to other countries.\nExample:\nLiberia exports a product to another country → export.\nLiberia buys a product from another country → import.\nInternational trade allows countries to obtain goods they may not produce efficiently themselves."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Economic Growth","x":"Economic growth means an increase in the production of goods and services in an economy over time.\nFor example, if Liberia produces significantly more goods and services this year than in previous years, its economy may have experienced economic growth."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Economic Development","x":"Economic development is broader than economic growth.\nIt involves improvements in people's living standards and economic and social well-being.\nIt can include improvements in:\n• Education\n• Healthcare\n• Employment\n• Infrastructure\n• Income\n• Access to basic services"},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Review of Basic Economic Concepts","x":"Definition / Explanation: Economics studies how individuals, businesses, governments, and societies use scarce resources to satisfy unlimited wants. The key ideas are scarcity, choice, opportunity cost, factors of production, demand, supply, production, and economic systems.\nSimple idea: Because we cannot have everything we want, we must make choices. Example: If you have $10 and want food, transportation, and phone data, you must decide how to spend the money."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Utility and Consumer Behaviour","x":"Definition / Explanation: Utility is the satisfaction or benefit a consumer receives from consuming a good or service. Consumer behaviour examines how people decide what to buy when their income and resources are limited.\nExample: A student may choose to spend money on lunch rather than entertainment because lunch gives greater satisfaction at that time."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility","x":"Definition / Explanation: The law states that as a person consumes additional units of the same good, the extra satisfaction gained from each additional unit generally decreases, assuming other factors remain unchanged.\nExample: The first bottle of cold water when you are very thirsty may give great satisfaction, but the fourth or fifth bottle may give much less additional satisfaction."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Consumer Equilibrium","x":"Definition / Explanation: Consumer equilibrium is the position where a consumer uses limited income to obtain the greatest possible satisfaction from available goods and services.\nSimple idea: A consumer tries to get the most satisfaction from the money available. Example: A student divides a limited allowance between food, transport, and school materials according to their priorities."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Elasticity of Demand","x":"Definition / Explanation: Elasticity of demand measures how responsive the quantity demanded of a good or service is to a change in price, income, or the price of another good.\nExample: If a small change in the price of a product causes a large change in the amount people buy, demand is relatively elastic."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Elasticity of Supply","x":"Definition / Explanation: Elasticity of supply measures how responsive the quantity supplied is to a change in price.\nExample: A factory may be able to increase production quickly when prices rise, while a farmer may need much more time to increase agricultural output."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Factors Affecting Demand and Supply","x":"Definition / Explanation: Demand can be affected by income, population, tastes, expectations, prices of substitutes and complements, and other factors. Supply can be affected by production costs, technology, taxes, subsidies, weather, and the number of producers.\nExample: A poor harvest can reduce the supply of rice and may put upward pressure on its price."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Production and Productivity","x":"Definition / Explanation: Production is the process of creating goods and services or adding value to them. Productivity measures how efficiently inputs such as labour, land, and capital are converted into output.\nExample: If two workers produce 100 units in one day and later produce 130 units with the same resources, their productivity has increased."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Short Run and Long Run Production","x":"Definition / Explanation: In the short run, at least one factor of production is fixed while other inputs can change. In the long run, firms can change all factors of production.\nExample: A bakery may be able to hire more workers quickly, but expanding the building may take much longer."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Total, Average and Marginal Product","x":"Definition / Explanation: Total product is the total amount produced. Average product is output per unit of a variable input. Marginal product is the additional output created by adding one more unit of a variable input.\nExample: If adding one more worker increases daily output from 100 to 115 units, the marginal product of that worker is 15 units."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Costs of Production","x":"Definition / Explanation: Production creates costs. Fixed costs do not change with output in the short run, while variable costs change as output changes. Total cost is the sum of fixed and variable costs.\nExample: Rent may remain the same whether a small bakery produces 100 or 200 loaves, while flour costs increase as more bread is produced."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Revenue and Profit","x":"Definition / Explanation: Revenue is the income a firm receives from selling goods and services. Profit is the amount left after total costs are subtracted from total revenue.\nFormula: Profit = Total Revenue − Total Cost. Example: If a business receives $5,000 and spends $3,500, its profit is $1,500."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Economies and Diseconomies of Scale","x":"Definition / Explanation: Economies of scale occur when a firm expands and its average cost of production falls. Diseconomies of scale occur when a firm becomes so large that average costs begin to rise.\nExample: A large producer may buy raw materials in bulk at lower prices, but an extremely large organization may become difficult to manage efficiently."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Business Organization and Forms of Business","x":"Definition / Explanation: Businesses can be organized in different ways, including sole proprietorships, partnerships, corporations, and cooperatives. Each form has different ownership, liability, financing, and management characteristics.\nExample: A small shop may begin as a sole proprietorship, while a large company may operate as a corporation with many owners."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Perfect Competition","x":"Definition / Explanation: Perfect competition describes a market with many buyers and sellers, standardized products, relatively free entry and exit, and firms with little individual control over market price.\nExample: Agricultural markets can sometimes resemble competitive markets because many producers may sell similar products."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Monopoly","x":"Definition / Explanation: A monopoly exists when one firm is the dominant or sole supplier of a product or service and significant barriers prevent competitors from entering the market.\nA monopolist may have considerable influence over price, although demand, regulation, and other constraints still matter."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Monopolistic Competition","x":"Definition / Explanation: Monopolistic competition is a market with many firms selling similar but differentiated products. Firms compete through price, quality, branding, location, and advertising.\nExample: Restaurants may sell similar types of food while trying to distinguish themselves through taste, service, location, or reputation."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Oligopoly","x":"Definition / Explanation: An oligopoly is a market dominated by a small number of major firms. Because there are few large competitors, each firm's decisions can affect the others.\nExample: In a market with only a few major telecommunications companies, a pricing decision by one company may influence the strategies of the others."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Price Discrimination","x":"Definition / Explanation: Price discrimination occurs when a seller charges different prices to different consumers or groups for the same or similar product, when the conditions for doing so exist.\nExample: A business may offer different prices to students, adults, or other customer groups."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Market Failure","x":"Definition / Explanation: Market failure occurs when the operation of a market does not lead to an efficient or socially desirable allocation of resources.\nExamples include pollution, under-provision of public goods, information problems, and market power."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Externalities","x":"Definition / Explanation: An externality is a cost or benefit created by an economic activity that affects people who are not directly involved in the transaction.\nExample: A factory may create pollution that harms nearby residents. This is a negative externality."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Public Goods","x":"Definition / Explanation: Public goods are goods that are generally non-rival and difficult to exclude people from using. Markets may provide too little of them, creating a role for government.\nExamples can include certain forms of public safety, street lighting, and national defence."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Government Intervention in the Economy","x":"Definition / Explanation: Government intervention means government actions that influence economic activity. Governments may intervene to correct market failures, protect consumers, provide public services, promote stability, and improve equity.\nExamples include taxes, subsidies, regulations, public spending, and price controls."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Price Controls","x":"Definition / Explanation: Price controls are government-imposed limits on prices. A maximum price, or price ceiling, limits how high a price can rise. A minimum price, or price floor, limits how low a price can fall.\nExample: A government may regulate the price of an essential good in an attempt to make it more affordable."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Taxation","x":"Definition / Explanation: Taxation is the collection of compulsory payments by government from individuals and businesses. Taxes provide revenue for public services and can also influence economic behaviour.\nExamples include income taxes, business taxes, sales-related taxes, and customs duties."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Subsidies","x":"Definition / Explanation: A subsidy is financial support provided by government to producers or consumers to reduce costs, encourage production or consumption, or achieve a policy objective.\nExample: Government support to farmers can reduce production costs and encourage food production."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Consumer and Producer Surplus","x":"Definition / Explanation: Consumer surplus is the difference between what consumers are willing to pay and what they actually pay. Producer surplus is the difference between the price producers receive and the minimum price they would accept.\nThese concepts help economists understand some of the benefits created by market exchanges."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Income Distribution and Inequality","x":"Definition / Explanation: Income distribution describes how income is shared among people or households. Income inequality occurs when income is distributed unevenly.\nGovernments may use taxation, social programmes, education, employment policies, and other measures to address severe inequality."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Review of National Income","x":"Definition / Explanation: National income measures and related indicators help economists understand the size and performance of an economy. Important concepts include GDP, GNP, national income, and per capita income.\nExample: GDP measures the value of final goods and services produced within a country's borders over a period."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Methods of Measuring National Income","x":"Definition / Explanation: National income can be measured using the output or production approach, the income approach, and the expenditure approach. In principle, the three approaches measure the same overall economic activity from different perspectives.\nExample: The expenditure approach adds spending on final goods and services, while the income approach considers income generated from production."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Circular Flow of Income","x":"Definition / Explanation: The circular flow model shows how income, spending, goods, and services move between households and firms, and can be expanded to include government, financial institutions, and the foreign sector.\nExample: Households provide labour to firms and receive income; households then use part of that income to buy goods and services from firms."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Consumption, Saving and Investment","x":"Definition / Explanation: Consumption is spending by households on goods and services. Saving is income that is not consumed. Investment is spending on productive assets such as machinery, buildings, and equipment.\nA healthy balance between consumption and investment can support both current living standards and future production."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Aggregate Demand and Aggregate Supply","x":"Definition / Explanation: Aggregate demand is total planned spending on domestically produced goods and services. Aggregate supply refers to the total amount of goods and services firms are willing to produce at different overall price levels.\nChanges in aggregate demand and supply can affect national output, employment, and the general price level."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Unemployment","x":"Definition / Explanation: Unemployment occurs when people who are able, available, and willing to work cannot find suitable employment.\nHigh unemployment can reduce household income, government revenue, and total economic output."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Types and Causes of Unemployment","x":"Definition / Explanation: Major types include frictional, structural, seasonal, and cyclical unemployment. Causes can include job transitions, changes in technology and skills, seasonal activity, and economic downturns.\nExample: A worker whose skills no longer match available jobs may experience structural unemployment."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Inflation and Deflation","x":"Definition / Explanation: Inflation is a sustained increase in the general price level. Deflation is a sustained decrease in the general price level.\nInflation can reduce purchasing power when incomes do not rise as quickly as prices. Example: If food and transportation prices rise continuously, the same amount of money buys less."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Fiscal Policy","x":"Definition / Explanation: Fiscal policy refers to government decisions about taxation and government spending used to influence economic activity.\nDuring a weak economy, government may increase spending or reduce certain taxes to support demand; during excessive demand, it may take the opposite approach."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Monetary Policy","x":"Definition / Explanation: Monetary policy involves actions by a central bank to influence money, credit, liquidity, and interest rates in order to pursue objectives such as price stability and economic stability.\nExample: Changes in interest rates can influence borrowing, saving, consumption, and investment."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Government Budget and Public Debt","x":"Definition / Explanation: A government budget presents planned revenue and expenditure. A budget deficit occurs when planned expenditure exceeds revenue. Public debt is the accumulated borrowing owed by government.\nBorrowing can finance important investments, but high or poorly managed debt can create financial pressures."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Balance of Payments","x":"Definition / Explanation: The balance of payments records a country's economic transactions with the rest of the world during a given period. It includes accounts such as the current account and financial account.\nIt helps a country understand how money flows into and out of the economy through trade, income, transfers, and financial transactions."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Exchange Rates","x":"Definition / Explanation: An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency.\nExample: The exchange rate between the Liberian dollar and U.S. dollar affects the local-currency cost of imported goods and the value of export earnings."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"International Trade","x":"Definition / Explanation: International trade is the exchange of goods and services between countries. Imports are goods and services purchased from abroad, while exports are goods and services sold abroad.\nExample: Liberia importing machinery is an import; Liberia selling a product to another country is an export."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Comparative Advantage","x":"Definition / Explanation: Comparative advantage explains how countries can benefit from specializing in goods and services they can produce at a lower opportunity cost relative to other goods.\nThe principle shows why trade can benefit both sides even when one country is more productive in producing many goods."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Trade Barriers and Protectionism","x":"Definition / Explanation: Trade barriers are restrictions on international trade. Examples include tariffs, quotas, import licences, and other restrictions. Protectionism refers to policies designed to protect domestic industries from foreign competition.\nGovernments may use protection for reasons such as supporting new industries, protecting jobs, or responding to strategic concerns, although protection can also raise prices or reduce competition."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Economic Growth and Development","x":"Definition / Explanation: Economic growth is an increase in the production of goods and services over time. Economic development is broader and includes improvements in living standards, health, education, opportunities, and economic and social well-being.\nA country can experience growth without all citizens experiencing equal improvements in living standards, so development considers more than output alone."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Poverty and Inequality","x":"Definition / Explanation: Poverty means lacking sufficient resources to meet basic needs. Inequality refers to differences in income, wealth, opportunities, or access to resources.\nReducing poverty and severe inequality may involve better education, healthcare, employment opportunities, infrastructure, social protection, and inclusive economic policies."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Sustainable Economic Development","x":"Definition / Explanation: Sustainable economic development seeks to improve living standards and economic opportunities while protecting natural resources and the environment for future generations.\nExample: Expanding agriculture while protecting forests, soil, water, and biodiversity supports more sustainable development."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Economic Challenges and Opportunities in Liberia","x":"Definition / Explanation: This topic applies Grade 11 economic concepts to Liberia. Students examine issues such as unemployment, inflation, agriculture, infrastructure, education, entrepreneurship, natural resources, trade, exchange rates, poverty, inequality, and economic development.\nLiberia also has opportunities in agriculture, fisheries, tourism, natural resources, small business, technology, education, and human-capital development. The goal is to use economic reasoning to understand challenges and identify practical opportunities."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Review of Grade 11 Microeconomics","x":"A review of consumer behaviour, production, costs, revenue, market structures, market failure, and government intervention."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Advanced Demand and Supply Analysis","x":"Students examine in greater detail how changes in income, preferences, technology, production costs, and other factors shift demand and supply."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Elasticity: Advanced Applications","x":"Students apply price, income, and cross elasticity to real business and economic decisions, including pricing and production."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Consumer Choice Theory","x":"This explains how consumers choose combinations of goods to maximize satisfaction given their limited income and the prices of goods."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Indifference Curves and Budget Lines","x":"Indifference curves show combinations of goods that give consumers equal satisfaction. A budget line shows what combinations consumers can afford."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Production Function","x":"A production function shows the relationship between inputs such as labour and capital and the output produced by a firm."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Law of Variable Proportions","x":"This explains what happens to output when one factor is increased while other factors remain fixed."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Returns to Scale","x":"Returns to scale examine what happens to output when all inputs are increased together."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Short-Run and Long-Run Costs","x":"Students examine fixed costs, variable costs, average costs, marginal costs, and how costs behave when production changes."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Revenue and Profit Maximization","x":"Students learn how firms calculate revenue and determine the level of production that can maximize profit."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Firm Equilibrium","x":"Firm equilibrium is the point where a firm has no reason to change its level of output because it is achieving its best possible economic position under the given conditions."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Market Structures: Advanced Analysis","x":"Students compare perfect competition, monopoly, monopolistic competition, and oligopoly using price, output, competition, and efficiency."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Price and Output Determination Under Perfect Competition","x":"Students examine how market demand and supply determine price and how individual firms decide their output."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Price and Output Determination Under Monopoly","x":"Students examine how a monopolist determines output and price and the effects of monopoly power on consumers and society."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Monopolistic Competition and Oligopoly","x":"Students study product differentiation, advertising, strategic behaviour, barriers to entry, and competition among a small number of firms."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Game Theory and Strategic Business Decisions","x":"Game theory examines how the decisions of one firm or individual can depend on the expected decisions of others."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"National Income Accounting","x":"Students learn how economists measure national output and income using GDP, GNP, national income, and related measures."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Circular Flow and Macroeconomic Equilibrium","x":"Students examine how households, firms, government, financial institutions, and the foreign sector interact and how an economy can reach equilibrium."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Consumption and Saving Functions","x":"This topic examines how household income affects spending and saving decisions."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Investment and Interest Rates","x":"Students learn why businesses invest and how interest rates can influence investment decisions."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"The Multiplier","x":"The multiplier explains how an initial change in spending can lead to a larger overall change in national income and output."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Aggregate Demand and Aggregate Supply","x":"Students examine total spending and total production in an economy and how they influence national output and prices."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Business Cycles","x":"Business cycles are recurring changes in economic activity, including expansion, peak, contraction, and recovery."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Inflation: Causes, Effects and Control","x":"Students examine different causes of inflation, its effects on households and businesses, and policies used to control it."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Unemployment: Causes, Effects and Solutions","x":"Students examine different types of unemployment, their economic and social effects, and possible government solutions."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Fiscal Policy and Government Stabilization","x":"Students study how government spending and taxation can be used to influence economic activity and stabilize the economy."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Monetary Policy and Central Banking","x":"Students examine how a central bank uses monetary policy to influence interest rates, credit, money, inflation, and economic activity."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Money Supply and Credit Creation","x":"This explains how money enters the economy and how commercial banks can create deposits through lending."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Interest Rates and Economic Activity","x":"Interest rates affect borrowing, saving, investment, consumption, and therefore overall economic activity."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Government Budget Deficits and Public Debt","x":"Students examine why governments borrow, how deficits arise, and the possible benefits and risks of public debt."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Stabilization Policies","x":"Students study the combination of fiscal, monetary, and other policies governments can use to reduce economic instability."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Trade-Offs Between Inflation and Unemployment","x":"Students examine situations where policies designed to reduce one economic problem may create pressure on another."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Growth Theory","x":"Students study theories explaining how economies increase their productive capacity and output over time."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Development Theory","x":"Development theory examines how countries improve living standards, reduce poverty, increase productivity, and transform their economies."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Indicators of Economic Development","x":"Students examine indicators such as GDP per capita, life expectancy, literacy, poverty rates, employment, and access to basic services."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Human Capital and Economic Development","x":"Human capital refers to the knowledge, skills, health, and abilities of people. Education and healthcare can increase productivity and development."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Poverty, Inequality and Redistribution","x":"Students examine why poverty and inequality exist and how taxation, social programmes, education, and employment policies can affect income distribution."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Population and Economic Development","x":"Students study how population growth, age structure, migration, and population density can affect economic development."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Agriculture and Industrial Development","x":"Agriculture can provide food, employment, exports, and raw materials, while industrial development can increase productivity, jobs, and economic diversification."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Natural Resources and Economic Development","x":"Students examine how resources such as minerals, forests, fisheries, and oil can contribute to development and the risks of poor resource management."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Sustainable Development and Environmental Economics","x":"This topic examines how economic growth can occur while protecting natural resources and the environment for future generations."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"International Trade Theory","x":"Students study why countries trade, what determines trade patterns, and how international trade can create economic benefits."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Comparative and Absolute Advantage","x":"Absolute advantage concerns producing more efficiently, while comparative advantage focuses on producing at a lower opportunity cost."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Trade Policies and Protectionism","x":"Students examine tariffs, quotas, subsidies, import restrictions, and the arguments for and against protecting domestic industries."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Balance of Payments and Exchange Rate Policy","x":"Students study international financial transactions and how exchange-rate policies can influence imports, exports, inflation, and economic stability."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Globalization and Developing Economies","x":"Globalization connects countries through trade, investment, technology, finance, and communication. Students examine its benefits and challenges for developing countries."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Regional Economic Integration","x":"Countries may cooperate economically by reducing trade barriers and coordinating policies. Students examine how regional integration can increase trade and investment."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"International Financial Institutions and Development","x":"Students learn about organizations such as the World Bank and International Monetary Fund (IMF) and their roles in international finance and development."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Liberia's Economic Structure and Major Sectors","x":"Students examine Liberia's major economic sectors, including agriculture, mining, services, trade, fisheries, manufacturing, and small businesses."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Liberia's Economic Challenges and Development Opportunities","x":"Students analyze challenges such as unemployment, infrastructure gaps, poverty, limited industrialization, and skills shortages, while examining opportunities for growth."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Policy in Liberia","x":"Students apply economic theory to government decisions involving taxation, spending, monetary policy, trade, investment, employment, and development."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Entrepreneurship and Private-Sector Development","x":"Students learn how entrepreneurs create businesses, jobs, innovation, and economic opportunities."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Youth Employment and Economic Development","x":"This topic examines youth unemployment and underemployment and explores how education, skills training, entrepreneurship, investment, and job creation can improve opportunities."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"The Future of Liberia's Economy","x":"Students use everything they have learned to think critically about Liberia's future economic opportunities, challenges, policies, and development priorities."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Data Interpretation","x":"Students learn how to read and interpret economic information presented in tables, statistics, reports, and datasets."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Tables, Charts and Graphs in Economics","x":"Students learn how to construct, read, and analyze economic graphs such as demand and supply curves, cost curves, and economic growth charts."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Calculations and Formulae","x":"Students practice calculations involving percentages, elasticity, costs, revenue, profit, national income, inflation, and other economic measures."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Interpreting Economic Statistics","x":"Students learn how to use economic statistics carefully and understand what numbers reveal—and what they do not reveal—about an economy."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Case Studies","x":"Students apply economic theories to real situations involving individuals, businesses, governments, Liberia, and other countries."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Essay Writing in Economics","x":"Students learn how to construct strong Economics answers using definitions, explanations, examples, diagrams, analysis, evaluation, and conclusions."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Economic Research Methods","x":"Students learn how to identify an economic problem, collect information, analyze evidence, and present findings."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Applying Economics to Real-Life Problems","x":"Students use economic concepts to understand everyday issues such as prices, unemployment, business decisions, transportation, education, and household budgeting."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Current Economic Issues","x":"Students discuss major economic issues affecting Liberia and the world and apply economic theories to understand them."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Final Grade 12 Economics Revision","x":"A complete review of the major concepts, theories, diagrams, calculations, definitions, and applications covered throughout Grade 12."},{"a":"eco","g":12,"t":"Mock Economics Examination","x":"Students complete practice examinations designed to test their knowledge, calculations, analysis, essay writing, and ability to apply economic concepts."},{"a":"eco","g":0,"t":"The three-year Economics journey","x":"Grade 10 is the Foundation: understand Economics. Grade 11 is Application: understand how consumers, firms, markets and governments operate. Grade 12 is Advanced analysis: analyze economic problems, policies, development and real-world issues."},{"a":"eco","g":10,"t":"Big picture of Grade 10 Economics","x":"Unlimited wants -> Scarcity -> Choice -> Opportunity cost -> Factors of production -> Production -> Demand + Supply -> Price -> Money + Banking -> Inflation + National Income -> Trade -> Economic Growth and Development. People have unlimited wants, resources are limited, so choices must be made about how resources are used."},{"a":"eco","g":11,"t":"Big picture of Grade 11 Economics","x":"Consumer behaviour -> Production -> Costs -> Revenue -> Firms -> Market structures -> Market failure -> Government intervention -> National income -> Unemployment -> Inflation -> Fiscal policy -> Monetary policy -> International trade -> Exchange rates -> Economic growth and development. The central idea remains the same: resources are limited, wants are unlimited, and economic decisions must be made about how resources are allocated."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 1: Numbers and Basic Operations","x":"Students use addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, order of operations, positive and negative numbers, and word problems. Economic uses include income, expenditure, balances, production, and sales.\nWorked example:\nA student receives $100 and spends $65 on food and transportation. Remaining money = $100 − $65 = $35. Answer: $35 remains."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 2: Fractions","x":"A fraction represents part of a whole. Students learn proper and improper fractions, mixed numbers, and the four basic operations with fractions.\nWorked example:\nIf 3 out of 10 students are absent, the fraction absent is 3/10. As a percentage: (3 ÷ 10) × 100 = 30%."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 3: Decimals","x":"Students add, subtract, multiply, divide, round decimals, and apply decimals to money calculations.\nWorked example:\nA student has $100 and spends $35.50. Remaining = $100 − $35.50 = $64.50."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 4: Percentages","x":"Percentage means 'out of 100.' Students convert fractions and decimals to percentages and calculate percentage increases, decreases, and changes.\nFormula: Percentage = (Part ÷ Whole) × 100; Percentage Change = ((New − Old) ÷ Old) × 100.\nWorked example:\nA class has 50 students and 30 are boys. Percentage of boys = (30 ÷ 50) × 100 = 60%."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 5: Ratio and Proportion","x":"A ratio compares quantities. Proportion states that two ratios are equal. Students simplify ratios and solve direct-proportion problems.\nWorked example:\nThere are 20 boys and 10 girls. Boys:girls = 20:10 = 2:1. If 5 workers produce 100 units at the same rate, 10 workers produce 200 units."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 6: Averages","x":"Students learn mean, median, mode, range, and simple weighted averages. These are used for average income, prices, production, and expenditure.\nFormula: Mean = Total ÷ Number of Values.\nWorked example:\nScores are 60, 70, 80, and 90. Mean = (60 + 70 + 80 + 90) ÷ 4 = 300 ÷ 4 = 75. Median = 75; there is no repeated value, so there is no mode."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 7: Profit and Loss","x":"Cost price is the buying price. Selling price is the price received from selling. Profit occurs when selling price is greater than cost price; loss occurs when it is lower.\nFormula: Profit = SP − CP; Loss = CP − SP; Profit% = (Profit ÷ CP) × 100; Loss% = (Loss ÷ CP) × 100.\nWorked example:\nA trader buys a bag for $50 and sells it for $65. Profit = $65 − $50 = $15. Profit% = ($15 ÷ $50) × 100 = 30%."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 8: Discount","x":"A discount is a reduction from the original price.\nFormula: Discount = Original Price × Discount Rate ÷ 100; Sale Price = Original Price − Discount.\nWorked example:\nA shirt costs $100 and has a 20% discount. Discount = $100 × 20/100 = $20. Sale price = $100 − $20 = $80."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 9: Simple Interest","x":"Simple interest is interest calculated on the original principal for the stated time.\nFormula: I = (P × R × T) ÷ 100; Amount = P + I.\nWorked example:\nFor P = $1,000, R = 5%, T = 2 years: I = (1,000 × 5 × 2) ÷ 100 = $100. Amount = $1,100."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 10: Tax","x":"Tax is money paid to government according to applicable tax rules. Students calculate tax and income after tax.\nFormula: Tax = Income × Tax Rate ÷ 100; After-tax Income = Income − Tax.\nWorked example:\nIncome = $500 and tax rate = 10%. Tax = $500 × 10/100 = $50. After-tax income = $450."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 11: Wages and Salaries","x":"Students calculate hourly, daily, weekly, and monthly earnings, including simple overtime and commission examples.\nFormula: Wage = Rate × Hours.\nWorked example:\nA worker earns $5 per hour and works 8 hours. Wage = $5 × 8 = $40."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 12: Budgeting","x":"A budget compares planned income with planned spending and savings. Students identify surplus, deficit, and remaining balances.\nFormula: Balance = Income − Total Expenses.\nWorked example:\nIncome = $300. Expenses are $100 food, $50 transport, $70 school, and $20 airtime. Total expenses = $240. Balance = $300 − $240 = $60."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 13: Revenue and Cost","x":"Revenue is money received from sales. Students use price and quantity to calculate total revenue.\nFormula: TR = Price × Quantity.\nWorked example:\nA business sells 100 products at $5 each. TR = $5 × 100 = $500."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 14: Fixed and Variable Costs","x":"Fixed costs generally do not change with production in the short run, while variable costs change with production.\nFormula: TC = FC + VC.\nWorked example:\nFixed cost = $200 and variable cost = $300. Total cost = $200 + $300 = $500."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 15: Average Cost","x":"Average cost is the cost per unit of output.\nFormula: AC = TC ÷ Quantity.\nWorked example:\nTotal cost = $1,000 and output = 200 units. AC = $1,000 ÷ 200 = $5 per unit."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 16: Production","x":"Students learn total product, average product, marginal product, production tables, and productivity.\nFormula: Marginal Product = Change in Total Product ÷ Change in Input.\nWorked example:\nA factory's output rises from 100 to 120 units when one additional worker is added. Marginal product of that additional worker = 120 − 100 = 20 units."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 17: Demand","x":"Demand is the quantity consumers are willing and able to buy at different prices. Students read demand schedules and understand the usual inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded.\nWorked example:\nIf price rises from $10 to $20 while quantity demanded falls from 80 to 40 units, the table shows a decrease in quantity demanded as price rises."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 18: Supply","x":"Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices. Students read supply schedules and understand the usual positive relationship between price and quantity supplied.\nWorked example:\nIf price rises from $10 to $20 while quantity supplied rises from 40 to 80 units, quantity supplied increased as price increased."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 19: Market Equilibrium","x":"Equilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\nFormula: Equilibrium condition: QD = QS.\nWorked example:\nAt $15, QD = 60 and QS = 60. Therefore equilibrium price = $15 and equilibrium quantity = 60 units."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 20: Graphs","x":"Students identify axes, plot points, read values, and interpret demand, supply, production, revenue, and cost graphs. In a basic demand/supply graph, price is commonly placed on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis.\nWorked example:\nTo graph a demand schedule with points (Q=100, P=$5), (Q=80, P=$10), and (Q=60, P=$15), plot quantity on the horizontal axis and price on the vertical axis, then connect the points to show the demand relationship."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 21: Inflation","x":"Inflation is a general increase in prices over time.\nFormula: Inflation Rate = ((New Price − Old Price) ÷ Old Price) × 100.\nWorked example:\nA basket costs $100 last year and $110 this year. Inflation rate = ($10 ÷ $100) × 100 = 10%."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 22: Population Growth","x":"Population growth measures how much population changes over a period.\nFormula: Growth Rate = ((New Population − Old Population) ÷ Old Population) × 100.\nWorked example:\nPopulation rises from 100,000 to 105,000. Growth = 5,000. Growth rate = (5,000 ÷ 100,000) × 100 = 5%."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 23: Exchange Rates","x":"An exchange rate tells how much one currency is worth in another currency. Rates vary over time.\nFormula: Converted Amount = Amount of Currency × Exchange Rate.\nWorked example:\nIf 1 USD = 200 LD, then 5 USD = 5 × 200 = 1,000 LD."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 24: Basic Algebra","x":"Students learn variables, constants, expressions, equations, substitution, and solving for unknowns.\nWorked example:\nSolve 2x + 10 = 30. Subtract 10: 2x = 20. Divide by 2: x = 10."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Unit 25: Economic Word Problems","x":"Students identify the given information, determine what is asked, choose the correct formula, substitute values, calculate, and state the answer with the correct unit.\nWorked example:\nA trader buys 20 bags at $30 each and sells each for $40. Cost = 20 × $30 = $600. Revenue = 20 × $40 = $800. Profit = $800 − $600 = $200."},{"a":"math","g":10,"t":"Master formula sheet (Grade 10 Economic Mathematics)","x":"Percentage: Part ÷ Whole × 100\nPercentage Change: (New − Old) ÷ Old × 100\nProfit: SP − CP\nLoss: CP − SP\nProfit Percentage: Profit ÷ CP × 100\nLoss Percentage: Loss ÷ CP × 100\nRevenue: Price × Quantity\nTotal Cost: Fixed Cost + Variable Cost\nProfit: Total Revenue − Total Cost\nAverage: Total ÷ Number of Values\nAverage Cost: Total Cost ÷ Quantity\nSimple Interest: P × R × T ÷ 100\nAmount: P + I\nDiscount: Original Price × Discount Rate ÷ 100\nSale Price: Original Price − Discount\nTax: Income × Tax Rate ÷ 100\nAfter-tax Income: Income − Tax\nWage: Rate × Hours\nGrowth Rate: Increase ÷ Original × 100\nMarginal Product: Change in Total Product ÷ Change in Input\nEquilibrium: Quantity Demanded = Quantity Supplied\nExchange Conversion: Amount × Exchange Rate"},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 1: Algebraic Expressions and Equations","x":"Students simplify expressions, collect like terms, expand brackets, factor simple expressions, and solve linear equations.\nFormula: Distributive law: a(b+c)=ab+ac.\nWorked example:\nSimplify 3x + 5 + 2x − 1. Collect like terms: 3x+2x=5x and 5−1=4. Answer: 5x+4. Solve 2x+10=30: 2x=20, so x=10."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 2: Simultaneous Equations","x":"Two equations can be solved together to find two unknowns. This is useful for economic models involving two unknown quantities.\nFormula: Use substitution or elimination.\nWorked example:\nSolve x+y=10 and x−y=2. Adding gives 2x=12, so x=6. Then y=4. Answer: x=6, y=4."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 3: Indices and Exponents","x":"Students work with powers and laws of indices, including multiplication, division, zero powers, and negative powers.\nFormula: a^m × a^n=a^(m+n); a^m ÷ a^n=a^(m−n); a^0=1.\nWorked example:\nSimplify 2^3 × 2^2 = 2^5 = 32."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 4: Logarithms","x":"Logarithms are the inverse of exponentiation and are useful for solving growth and compound-interest problems.\nFormula: If a^x=b, then log_a(b)=x.\nWorked example:\nSolve 10^x=1,000. Since 10^3=1,000, x=3. Therefore log10(1,000)=3."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 5: Sequences and Series","x":"Students study arithmetic sequences and basic series, including finding terms and sums.\nFormula: Arithmetic nth term: a_n=a+(n−1)d; sum: S_n=n/2[2a+(n−1)d].\nWorked example:\nFind the 10th term of 2,5,8,... Here a=2 and d=3. a10=2+9(3)=29."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 6: Variation and Proportion","x":"Students use direct, inverse, and combined variation to model relationships.\nFormula: Direct: y=kx. Inverse: y=k/x.\nWorked example:\nIf y varies directly with x and y=12 when x=3, k=4. Therefore when x=5, y=4(5)=20."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 7: Functions and Graphs","x":"Students use functions to represent economic relationships such as cost, revenue, demand, and supply. They evaluate functions and interpret graphs.\nFormula: f(x)=expression in x.\nWorked example:\nIf C(q)=200+5q, find C(40). C(40)=200+5(40)=400. Total cost is $400."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 8: Compound Interest","x":"Compound interest adds interest to the balance so later interest is earned on previous interest.\nFormula: A=P(1+r)^t for annual compounding.\nWorked example:\nInvest $1,000 at 10% for 2 years. A=1,000(1.10)^2=$1,210. Compound interest=$210."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 9: Depreciation","x":"Depreciation measures the fall in value of an asset. Students may use straight-line or percentage depreciation.\nFormula: Straight-line annual depreciation=(Cost−Salvage Value)÷Useful Life.\nWorked example:\nA machine costs $10,000, has a $2,000 salvage value, and lasts 4 years. Annual depreciation=(10,000−2,000)÷4=$2,000."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 10: Annuities and Present Value","x":"Students are introduced to repeated equal payments and the idea that money received today has a different value from money received later.\nFormula: Present value of one future amount: PV=FV/(1+r)^t.\nWorked example:\nIf $1,210 will be received in 2 years at 10%, PV=1,210/(1.10)^2=$1,000."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 11: Percentages, Markups and Margins","x":"Students distinguish markup from profit margin and calculate price changes.\nFormula: Markup%=Profit÷Cost×100; Margin%=Profit÷Selling Price×100.\nWorked example:\nCost=$80 and selling price=$100. Profit=$20. Markup=20/80×100=25%. Margin=20/100×100=20%."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 12: Commission, Wages and Overtime","x":"Students calculate commission, regular wages, overtime, and combined earnings.\nFormula: Commission=Sales×Commission Rate.\nWorked example:\nA salesperson sells $2,000 at 5% commission. Commission=$2,000×0.05=$100."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 13: Cost, Revenue and Profit Functions","x":"Students represent business relationships with functions and calculate total cost, revenue, and profit.\nFormula: TR(q)=P(q)q; Profit(q)=TR(q)−TC(q).\nWorked example:\nIf TR(q)=10q and TC(q)=200+4q, then Profit(q)=10q−(200+4q)=6q−200."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 14: Break-Even Analysis","x":"Break-even is the output level where total revenue equals total cost and profit is zero.\nFormula: Break-even quantity = Fixed Cost ÷ (Price−Variable Cost per Unit).\nWorked example:\nFixed cost=$1,000, price=$20, variable cost=$10. Break-even=1,000/(20−10)=100 units."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 15: Budgeting and Cash Flow","x":"Students prepare simple budgets, identify surpluses/deficits, and calculate cash inflows and outflows.\nFormula: Net Cash Flow=Cash Inflows−Cash Outflows.\nWorked example:\nInflows=$5,000 and outflows=$4,200. Net cash flow=$800."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 16: Demand and Supply Functions","x":"Students use equations to represent demand and supply and calculate quantities at different prices.\nFormula: Example forms: Qd=a−bP; Qs=c+dP.\nWorked example:\nIf Qd=100−2P and P=20, Qd=100−40=60 units."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 17: Market Equilibrium","x":"Equilibrium is found by setting quantity demanded equal to quantity supplied.\nFormula: Set Qd=Qs.\nWorked example:\nIf Qd=100−2P and Qs=20+2P: 100−2P=20+2P, so 80=4P, P=20. Q=60."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 18: Elasticity of Demand","x":"Price elasticity of demand measures how responsive quantity demanded is to a price change.\nFormula: PED=% change in Qd ÷ % change in P.\nWorked example:\nPrice rises 10% and quantity demanded falls 20%. PED=−20%/10%=−2. In absolute value, elasticity is 2, so demand is elastic."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 19: Elasticity of Supply","x":"Price elasticity of supply measures the responsiveness of quantity supplied to price.\nFormula: PES=% change in Qs ÷ % change in P.\nWorked example:\nPrice rises 10% and quantity supplied rises 15%. PES=15%/10%=1.5."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 20: Marginal Analysis","x":"Marginal analysis examines the additional benefit or cost from one more unit.\nFormula: Marginal value = Change in total value ÷ Change in quantity.\nWorked example:\nTotal revenue rises from $500 to $560 when output rises from 50 to 60 units. Marginal revenue=(560−500)/(60−50)=$6 per unit."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 21: Average and Marginal Cost","x":"Students distinguish average cost from marginal cost and interpret cost changes.\nFormula: AC=TC/Q; MC=Change in TC÷Change in Q.\nWorked example:\nCost rises from $500 for 100 units to $560 for 110 units. MC=(560−500)/(110−100)=$6 per additional unit."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 22: National Income and Growth Calculations","x":"Students calculate percentage growth and basic changes in national output or income.\nFormula: Growth Rate=(New−Old)÷Old×100.\nWorked example:\nNational output rises from $20 billion to $21 billion. Growth=(1/20)×100=5%."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 23: Inflation and Price Indices","x":"Students calculate inflation from price data and use a simple price index.\nFormula: Price Index=(Cost of Basket in Current Year ÷ Cost in Base Year)×100.\nWorked example:\nA basket costs $120 in the base year and $132 now. Index=(132/120)×100=110. This represents a 10% increase relative to the base."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 24: Probability and Economic Decisions","x":"Probability measures the chance of an event and can support simple risk calculations.\nFormula: P(Event)=Favorable Outcomes÷Total Outcomes.\nWorked example:\nIf 3 of 10 equally likely outcomes represent a profitable result, P(profit)=3/10=0.3=30%."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 25: Statistics for Economics","x":"Students work with mean, median, mode, range, frequency tables, and basic interpretation of data.\nFormula: Mean=Σx/n.\nWorked example:\nIncome values are $100, $150, $200, and $250. Mean=(100+150+200+250)/4=$175."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 26: Index Numbers","x":"Index numbers compare a variable with a base period, usually setting the base to 100.\nFormula: Index=(Current Value÷Base Value)×100.\nWorked example:\nPrice rises from $50 to $60. Index=(60/50)×100=120. The price is 20% above the base."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Unit 27: Economic Word Problems and Data Interpretation","x":"Students combine mathematics with economic reasoning: identify variables, choose formulas, calculate, interpret, and explain results.\nFormula: General method: Given → Required → Formula → Substitute → Calculate → Interpret.\nWorked example:\nA business has fixed cost $500, price $15, and variable cost $5. Break-even=500/(15−5)=50 units. The business must sell 50 units to cover total cost."},{"a":"math","g":11,"t":"Master formula sheet (Grade 11 Economic Mathematics)","x":"Linear equation: ax+b=c → x=(c−b)/a\nDirect variation: y=kx\nInverse variation: y=k/x\nArithmetic nth term: a_n=a+(n−1)d\nArithmetic series: S_n=n/2[2a+(n−1)d]\nCompound amount: A=P(1+r)^t\nCompound interest: CI=A−P\nPresent value: PV=FV/(1+r)^t\nStraight-line depreciation: (Cost−Salvage Value)÷Useful Life\nMarkup %: Profit÷Cost×100\nMargin %: Profit÷Selling Price×100\nCommission: Sales×Commission Rate\nTotal revenue: TR=P×Q\nProfit: TR−TC\nBreak-even quantity: FC÷(Price−Variable Cost per Unit)\nNet cash flow: Cash Inflows−Cash Outflows\nDemand function: Qd=a−bP\nSupply function: Qs=c+dP\nEquilibrium: Qd=Qs\nPED: %ΔQd÷%ΔP\nPES: %ΔQs÷%ΔP\nMarginal value: ΔTotal Value÷ΔQuantity\nAverage cost: TC÷Q\nMarginal cost: ΔTC÷ΔQ\nGrowth rate: (New−Old)÷Old×100\nPrice index: Current Value÷Base Value×100\nProbability: Favorable Outcomes÷Total Outcomes\nMean: Σx÷n"},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 1: Advanced Algebra and Equations","x":"Students solve linear and quadratic equations, simplify algebraic expressions, factor expressions, and use the quadratic formula.\nFormula: Quadratic formula: x = [−b ± √(b²−4ac)]/(2a).\nWorked example:\nSolve x²−5x+6=0. Factor: (x−2)(x−3)=0. Therefore x=2 or x=3."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 2: Simultaneous Equations and Matrices","x":"Students solve systems of equations and are introduced to matrices as a compact way of representing economic systems.\nFormula: For a 2×2 matrix [[a,b],[c,d]], determinant = ad−bc.\nWorked example:\nFor matrix [[2,1],[3,4]], determinant = (2×4)−(1×3)=5."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 3: Functions and Inverse Functions","x":"Students evaluate functions, find domains and ranges at an introductory level, and work with inverse functions.\nFormula: If f(x)=ax+b, then f⁻¹(x)=(x−b)/a.\nWorked example:\nIf f(x)=2x+4, let y=2x+4. Then x=(y−4)/2, so f⁻¹(x)=(x−4)/2."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 4: Exponential and Logarithmic Models","x":"Exponential models describe compound growth, population growth, and other percentage-growth processes.\nFormula: Growth model: A=P(1+r)^t; continuous model: A=Pe^(rt).\nWorked example:\nAn amount of $1,000 grows at 5% annually for 3 years: A=1,000(1.05)^3≈$1,157.63."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 5: Sequences, Series and Growth","x":"Students calculate arithmetic and geometric sequences and use them to model repeated growth.\nFormula: Geometric nth term: a_n=ar^(n−1); sum: S_n=a(r^n−1)/(r−1), r≠1.\nWorked example:\nFor 100, 110, 121,..., a=100 and r=1.10. The 4th term is 100(1.10)^3=$133.10."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 6: Differentiation","x":"Differentiation measures the rate at which one variable changes with another. It is central to marginal economic analysis.\nFormula: Power rule: d(x^n)/dx = nx^(n−1).\nWorked example:\nIf C(q)=q²+10q+100, then C'(q)=2q+10."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 7: Applications of Differentiation in Economics","x":"Students use derivatives to find marginal cost, marginal revenue, marginal profit, and possible maximum or minimum values.\nFormula: Marginal cost MC=C'(q); marginal revenue MR=R'(q); marginal profit=π'(q).\nWorked example:\nIf R(q)=20q−q², then MR=20−2q. At q=5, MR=10."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 8: Integration and Economic Applications","x":"Integration reverses differentiation and can be used to recover total functions from marginal functions when a starting value is known.\nFormula: ∫x^n dx = x^(n+1)/(n+1)+C, n≠−1.\nWorked example:\nIf MC=2q+10 and fixed cost is $100, total cost is ∫(2q+10)dq=q²+10q+C. Since C(0)=100, C=q²+10q+100."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 9: Compound Interest and Effective Rates","x":"Students distinguish nominal and effective rates and compare different compounding periods.\nFormula: A=P(1+r/n)^(nt).\nWorked example:\nInvest $1,000 at 12% nominal annual interest compounded monthly for 1 year: A=1000(1+0.12/12)^12≈$1,126.83."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 10: Annuities, Loans and Amortization","x":"Students calculate regular payments on loans and understand how interest and principal change over time.\nFormula: Loan payment: PMT = P r(1+r)^n / [(1+r)^n−1].\nWorked example:\nFor a $1,000 loan at 1% per month for 12 months, PMT≈$88.85 per month."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 11: Depreciation and Investment Analysis","x":"Students compare straight-line and reducing-balance depreciation and evaluate simple investment returns.\nFormula: Reducing balance: V_t=V_0(1−r)^t.\nWorked example:\nAn asset worth $10,000 depreciates 20% annually. After 2 years: V=10,000(0.8)^2=$6,400."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 12: Cost, Revenue and Profit Models","x":"Students model business activity with functions and distinguish fixed, variable, average, and marginal measures.\nFormula: π(q)=R(q)−C(q); AC=C(q)/q.\nWorked example:\nIf R=30q and C=300+10q, then π=20q−300."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 13: Break-Even and Contribution Analysis","x":"Contribution is the amount each unit contributes toward fixed costs and then profit.\nFormula: Contribution per unit = Price−Variable Cost; Break-even = Fixed Cost/Contribution per Unit.\nWorked example:\nPrice=$25, variable cost=$15, fixed cost=$2,000. Contribution=$10; break-even=2,000/10=200 units."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 14: Optimization","x":"Students use algebra and calculus to maximize revenue/profit or minimize cost when an objective function is given.\nFormula: At an interior optimum, first derivative = 0; second derivative can help identify maximum/minimum.\nWorked example:\nIf π(q)=40q−q²−100, π'(q)=40−2q. Set to zero: q=20. Since π''(q)=−2, this is a maximum."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 15: Budgeting, Cash Flow and Financial Ratios","x":"Students prepare budgets and calculate liquidity, profitability, and simple efficiency ratios.\nFormula: Current Ratio = Current Assets ÷ Current Liabilities.\nWorked example:\nCurrent assets=$10,000 and current liabilities=$5,000. Current ratio=2. This means $2 of current assets for every $1 of current liabilities."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 16: Descriptive Statistics","x":"Students summarize economic data using mean, median, mode, range, frequency tables, and weighted averages.\nFormula: Mean = Σx/n; weighted mean = Σwx/Σw.\nWorked example:\nValues 10, 20, 30 have weights 1, 2, 1. Weighted mean=(10+40+30)/4=20."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 17: Variance and Standard Deviation","x":"Variance and standard deviation measure the spread of observations around the mean.\nFormula: Population variance σ²=Σ(x−μ)²/N; standard deviation σ=√σ².\nWorked example:\nFor 2,4,6, mean=4. Variance=[(−2)²+0²+2²]/3=8/3≈2.67; SD≈1.63."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 18: Probability and Expected Value","x":"Students calculate probabilities and expected monetary values for uncertain economic outcomes.\nFormula: E(X)=Σ[xP(x)].\nWorked example:\nA project earns $100 with probability .6 and loses $50 with probability .4. Expected value=.6(100)+.4(−50)=$40."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 19: Sampling and Data Interpretation","x":"Students distinguish population and sample, understand sampling methods, and interpret sample statistics cautiously.\nFormula: Sample mean: x̄=Σx/n.\nWorked example:\nA sample of 5 incomes is 100,120,140,160,180. Sample mean=700/5=$140."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 20: Correlation and Regression","x":"Correlation measures the strength/direction of linear association. Regression estimates a relationship for prediction.\nFormula: Simple regression: y=a+bx.\nWorked example:\nIf a=10 and b=2, predicted y when x=5 is y=10+2(5)=20."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 21: Index Numbers and Inflation","x":"Students construct simple price indices and calculate inflation rates from index values.\nFormula: Price Index=(Current Cost/Base Cost)×100; Inflation≈[(New Index−Old Index)/Old Index]×100.\nWorked example:\nIndex rises from 120 to 126. Inflation=(6/120)×100=5%."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 22: National Income and Macroeconomic Calculations","x":"Students work with GDP, growth rates, per-capita measures, and basic expenditure accounting.\nFormula: Per-capita GDP=GDP/Population; Growth Rate=(New−Old)/Old×100.\nWorked example:\nGDP=$5 billion and population=1 million. Per-capita GDP=$5,000."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 23: Elasticity and Economic Interpretation","x":"Students calculate price, income, and cross-price elasticity and interpret elastic, inelastic, and unit elastic responses.\nFormula: PED=%ΔQd/%ΔP; YED=%ΔQ/%ΔIncome; XED=%ΔQ_A/%ΔP_B.\nWorked example:\nIncome rises 10% and demand for a product rises 20%. YED=20/10=2, so demand is income elastic."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 24: Consumer and Producer Surplus","x":"Students use simple areas under demand and above supply curves to estimate surplus in a linear market.\nFormula: Consumer surplus for a linear demand curve can be calculated as 1/2 × base × height.\nWorked example:\nMaximum willingness to pay=$50, market price=$30, quantity=100. CS=1/2×100×(50−30)=$1,000."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 25: Marginal Concepts and Economic Decision Rules","x":"Students compare marginal benefit with marginal cost and use the rule that an activity is worthwhile when marginal benefit is at least marginal cost, subject to the model.\nFormula: Choose additional activity while MB≥MC; optimum often occurs where MB=MC.\nWorked example:\nIf MB=$12 and MC=$9 for one more unit, the additional unit has net benefit of $3."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 26: Linear Programming Basics","x":"Students optimize an objective subject to resource constraints using simple inequalities and feasible regions.\nFormula: Maximize Z=ax+by subject to resource constraints.\nWorked example:\nIf a business earns $5 per chair and $3 per table, then Z=5x+3y; the best feasible corner can be compared to maximize Z."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Unit 27: Comprehensive Economic Data Problems","x":"Students combine several skills: tables, percentages, averages, functions, graphs, elasticity, finance, and interpretation.\nFormula: General method: identify data → define variables → select formula/model → calculate → check → interpret.\nWorked example:\nA firm's revenue is $20,000 and cost is $15,000. Profit=$5,000. If revenue rises 10% and cost rises 5%, new revenue=$22,000, new cost=$15,750, new profit=$6,250."},{"a":"math","g":12,"t":"Master formula sheet (Grade 12 Economic Mathematics)","x":"Quadratic formula: x=[−b±√(b²−4ac)]/(2a)\n2×2 determinant: ad−bc\nInverse linear function: If f(x)=ax+b, f⁻¹(x)=(x−b)/a\nCompound growth: A=P(1+r)^t\nContinuous growth: A=Pe^(rt)\nGeometric nth term: a_n=ar^(n−1)\nGeometric series: S_n=a(r^n−1)/(r−1)\nPower derivative: d(x^n)/dx=nx^(n−1)\nMarginal cost: MC=C'(q)\nMarginal revenue: MR=R'(q)\nMarginal profit: Mπ=π'(q)\nBasic integration: ∫x^n dx=x^(n+1)/(n+1)+C\nCompound periodic rate: A=P(1+r/n)^(nt)\nLoan payment: PMT=Pr(1+r)^n/[(1+r)^n−1]\nReducing depreciation: V_t=V_0(1−r)^t\nProfit: π=TR−TC\nAverage cost: AC=TC/Q\nContribution/unit: Price−Variable Cost\nBreak-even quantity: Fixed Cost÷Contribution per Unit\nCurrent ratio: Current Assets÷Current Liabilities\nMean: Σx/n\nWeighted mean: Σwx/Σw\nPopulation variance: Σ(x−μ)²/N\nStandard deviation: √Variance\nExpected value: Σ[xP(x)]\nRegression line: y=a+bx\nPrice index: Current Cost÷Base Cost×100\nGrowth rate: (New−Old)÷Old×100\nPer-capita GDP: GDP÷Population\nPED: %ΔQd÷%ΔP\nYED: %ΔQ÷%ΔIncome\nXED: %ΔQ_A÷%ΔP_B\nConsumer surplus: 1/2×Base×Height\nEconomic optimum: Often MB=MC"},{"a":"math","g":0,"t":"General method for economic word problems","x":"Given -> Required -> Formula -> Substitute -> Calculate -> Interpret. Identify the given information, decide what is asked, choose the correct formula, substitute values, calculate, and state the answer with the correct unit and its economic meaning."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a positive mindset?","x":"A positive mindset is a way of thinking that focuses on possibilities, solutions, learning, and hope, even when things are difficult."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is a positive mindset important?","x":"It helps you face challenges, make better decisions, stay motivated, and keep working toward your goals."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can a positive mindset change my life?","x":"Yes. The way you think can influence how you respond to situations, the decisions you make, and the actions you take."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Is being positive the same as pretending everything is perfect?","x":"No. Being positive does not mean ignoring problems. It means recognizing the problem while believing you can work through it."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a negative mindset?","x":"A negative mindset is a pattern of thinking that focuses mainly on problems, limitations, fear, or reasons why something cannot be done."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can I change a negative mindset?","x":"Yes. You can develop healthier thinking habits by becoming aware of negative thoughts and replacing them with realistic and constructive thoughts."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is self-belief?","x":"Self-belief is confidence in your ability to learn, grow, make decisions, and work toward your goals."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I believe in myself?","x":"Believing in yourself gives you the courage to try, learn from mistakes, and continue when things become difficult."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when I doubt myself?","x":"Ask yourself what evidence supports the doubt, remember what you have already accomplished, and focus on the next step you can take."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can I succeed if I don't feel confident?","x":"Yes. You do not need to feel completely confident before taking action. Confidence often grows through practice and experience."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a growth mindset?","x":"A growth mindset is the belief that your abilities can improve through learning, practice, effort, and experience."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is the opposite of a growth mindset?","x":"A fixed mindset assumes that abilities are mostly unchangeable and that failure proves you are not capable."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can intelligence improve?","x":"Yes. People can develop knowledge, skills, and abilities through learning, practice, feedback, and experience."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I say when something is difficult?","x":"Instead of saying, 'I can't do this,' try saying, 'I can't do this yet, but I can learn.'"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What does 'I can't do it yet' mean?","x":"It means you recognize that you have not mastered something now but believe you can improve with learning and practice."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is effort important?","x":"Effort gives you the opportunity to practice, improve your skills, and learn from experience."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Does hard work guarantee success?","x":"No. Hard work is important, but success can also depend on planning, skills, opportunities, timing, resources, and other circumstances."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I keep trying?","x":"Continued effort gives you more opportunities to learn, improve, and discover better ways to reach your goal."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when I make a mistake?","x":"Accept the mistake, understand what caused it, learn from it, and decide what you will do differently next time."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Is making mistakes bad?","x":"Mistakes are not automatically bad. They can become valuable learning experiences when you reflect on them and improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is failure?","x":"Failure is an outcome that does not meet your expected goal."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Does failure mean I am a failure?","x":"No. A failed attempt is an event or result; it does not define your worth or your entire future."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How should I respond to failure?","x":"Give yourself time to process it, identify what you can learn, make a new plan, and try again when appropriate."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can failure teach me something?","x":"Yes. Failure can reveal mistakes, weaknesses, unexpected problems, and better strategies."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if I fail an examination?","x":"Don't give up. Review your mistakes, identify the topics you need to improve, change your study approach, and prepare for the next opportunity."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if people laugh at my failure?","x":"Remember that other people's opinions do not determine your potential. Learn from the experience and continue improving."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I turn failure into motivation?","x":"Ask yourself, 'What can I learn from this, and what will I do differently next time?'"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Should I be afraid of failure?","x":"It is normal to feel afraid of failure, but fear should not automatically stop you from trying."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is resilience?","x":"Resilience is the ability to recover, adapt, and continue moving forward after difficulties or setbacks."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I become more resilient?","x":"Develop healthy habits, learn from challenges, seek support when needed, and focus on what you can control."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stay positive during difficult times?","x":"Accept that the situation is difficult, focus on what you can control, seek support, and take one useful step at a time."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when life feels overwhelming?","x":"Pause, identify the most urgent problem, break it into smaller steps, and talk to someone you trust if you need support."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stop overthinking?","x":"Write down your concerns, separate facts from assumptions, focus on what you can control, and take a practical next step."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I control negative thoughts?","x":"Notice the thought, question whether it is completely true, and replace it with a more balanced and helpful way of thinking."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can I control every thought I have?","x":"No. Thoughts can appear automatically. You can learn how to respond to them rather than automatically believing or acting on them."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when I think, 'I can't succeed'?","x":"Ask yourself, 'What is one thing I can do today that moves me closer to my goal?'"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when I feel discouraged?","x":"Take a short break if needed, remind yourself why the goal matters, seek encouragement, and focus on the next manageable step."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stay hopeful?","x":"Look for realistic possibilities, remember that circumstances can change, and focus on actions that can improve your situation."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is optimism?","x":"Optimism is expecting that positive possibilities can exist while still recognizing difficulties and preparing realistically."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can optimism help students?","x":"Yes. It can help students approach challenges with greater persistence and look for ways to improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I build confidence?","x":"Set small goals, practice consistently, learn from mistakes, and recognize your progress."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stop comparing myself to others?","x":"Compare yourself mainly with your previous progress. Everyone has different circumstances, strengths, weaknesses, and starting points."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is comparison sometimes harmful?","x":"Constant comparison can make you focus on other people's results instead of your own growth."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when someone says I cannot succeed?","x":"Listen for useful information, but do not automatically accept someone's negative prediction about your future."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I handle criticism?","x":"Listen calmly, identify whether the criticism contains useful information, and use what helps you improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Is all criticism bad?","x":"No. Constructive criticism can help you identify weaknesses and improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is constructive criticism?","x":"It is feedback that identifies a problem and gives useful information that can help you improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I become mentally stronger?","x":"Develop healthy habits, face manageable challenges, learn from setbacks, and practice responding calmly to difficult situations."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What does being strong-minded mean?","x":"It means being able to think clearly, stay committed to your values and goals, and respond thoughtfully to challenges."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Does being mentally strong mean never feeling sad?","x":"No. Mental strength includes accepting difficult emotions and finding healthy ways to deal with them."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can a positive mindset help me in school?","x":"It can help you approach difficult subjects as opportunities to learn rather than immediate proof that you cannot succeed."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when I don't understand a lesson?","x":"Ask questions, review the basics, practice examples, seek help from a teacher or classmate, and keep trying."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stay motivated to study?","x":"Connect your studies to your goals, create a realistic schedule, remove distractions, and reward consistent effort."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if I don't like a subject?","x":"Try to understand how the subject connects to your goals or everyday life, and focus on learning it one step at a time."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stop procrastinating?","x":"Break the task into a very small first step and start for a short period instead of waiting until you feel motivated."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is discipline?","x":"Discipline is the ability to follow through on important actions even when you do not feel motivated."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Is discipline more important than motivation?","x":"They work together. Motivation can help you start, while discipline helps you continue when motivation decreases."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I develop discipline?","x":"Start with small commitments, create routines, reduce distractions, and consistently follow through."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I focus while studying?","x":"Choose one task, put away unnecessary distractions, study in manageable periods, and take planned breaks."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do if I get a low grade?","x":"Do not use one grade to define yourself. Find out what went wrong, improve your study strategy, and prepare better for the next assessment."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a goal?","x":"A goal is something you intentionally want to achieve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why are goals important?","x":"Goals give you direction and help you decide what actions deserve your time and effort."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How do I set a good goal?","x":"Make it clear, realistic, measurable when possible, and connected to something that matters to you."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is the difference between a dream and a goal?","x":"A dream is something you hope for. A goal turns that hope into a specific target and action plan."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I write down my goals?","x":"Writing goals can make them clearer and give you something specific to review and track."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when my goal feels too big?","x":"Break it into smaller goals and focus on completing the next step."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if I don't achieve my goal?","x":"Review what happened, learn from the experience, adjust the plan if necessary, and decide whether to continue or change direction."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stay focused on my goals?","x":"Keep your goals visible, create a routine, track your progress, and remind yourself why the goal matters."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is perseverance?","x":"Perseverance is continuing to work toward something despite difficulties or setbacks."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is perseverance important?","x":"Meaningful goals often require time, practice, patience, and repeated effort."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What are positive habits?","x":"Positive habits are repeated behaviors that support your learning, health, relationships, discipline, and personal growth."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What positive habit can I start today?","x":"Choose one simple habit, such as reading for 15 minutes, planning your day, exercising, or reviewing your lessons."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I make positive thinking a habit?","x":"Practice noticing negative thinking, challenging unrealistic conclusions, and deliberately focusing on realistic solutions."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is gratitude important?","x":"Gratitude helps you notice and appreciate the good things, people, opportunities, and experiences in your life."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What are three things I can be grateful for?","x":"You can be grateful for your life, people who support you, opportunities to learn, or small positive experiences."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I start my day positively?","x":"Set an intention for the day, think about what matters, organize your priorities, and begin with a useful action."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I end my day positively?","x":"Think about what you learned, what went well, what you can improve, and what you want to accomplish tomorrow."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is consistency important?","x":"Small actions repeated consistently can produce meaningful improvement over time."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can small steps lead to big results?","x":"Yes. Many major achievements are built from small actions repeated over time."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I be patient with myself?","x":"Growth takes time. Being patient allows you to learn without expecting instant perfection."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I encourage another student?","x":"Listen to them, remind them of their strengths, encourage realistic action, and let them know they do not have to face every challenge alone."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should students encourage each other?","x":"A supportive environment can make students feel more motivated, confident, and willing to keep learning."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I be a positive influence?","x":"Treat people with respect, encourage others, take responsibility for your actions, and demonstrate positive habits."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when my friends are negative?","x":"Listen respectfully without joining harmful negativity. Encourage constructive thinking and protect your own mindset."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Should I avoid every negative person?","x":"Not necessarily. Some people may be struggling and need support. Set healthy boundaries when someone's behavior consistently harms you."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is kindness?","x":"Kindness is treating others with care, respect, and consideration."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is kindness part of a positive mindset?","x":"A positive mindset is not only about personal success. It also involves how you treat and support other people."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I deal with anger?","x":"Pause before reacting, identify what triggered the anger, breathe or take space, and communicate when you are calm."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I forgive someone?","x":"Acknowledge what happened, process your feelings, decide what boundaries you need, and choose whether to release resentment over time."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Does forgiveness mean forgetting?","x":"No. Forgiveness does not require pretending the event never happened or removing healthy boundaries."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What does 'Think Positive, Live Powerful' mean?","x":"It means developing constructive thinking and using that mindset to make responsible choices and take positive action."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can mindset solve every problem?","x":"No. A positive mindset cannot remove every problem. It can help you respond to problems with greater clarity, responsibility, and determination."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Is positive thinking enough to become successful?","x":"No. Positive thinking should be combined with knowledge, planning, discipline, action, learning, and persistence."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is the connection between mindset and action?","x":"Mindset influences how you interpret situations, while action turns your intentions into results."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why do our choices matter?","x":"Our choices can influence our habits, relationships, opportunities, and direction in life."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when I feel like giving up?","x":"Pause and identify why you want to give up. Seek support, reconsider your strategy, and decide whether to continue, adjust your approach, or take a healthy break."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is one powerful question I can ask myself?","x":"Ask: 'What can I do today to make my situation better?'"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is the most important thing about developing a positive mindset?","x":"A positive mindset is a practice. You develop it through repeated choices, reflection, learning, and action."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can positive thinking make me successful without action?","x":"No. Positive thinking is most useful when it leads to responsible decisions and consistent action."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is self-respect important?","x":"Self-respect helps you recognize your value and make choices that protect your dignity and well-being."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I respect myself?","x":"Set healthy boundaries, keep promises to yourself, learn from mistakes, and treat yourself with patience."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is courage?","x":"Courage is taking responsible action even when you feel afraid or uncertain."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can I be afraid and still be brave?","x":"Yes. Courage does not mean having no fear. It means choosing a constructive action despite fear."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I overcome fear of trying?","x":"Start with a manageable step, prepare yourself, accept that mistakes can happen, and focus on what you can control."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I take responsibility for my actions?","x":"Responsibility helps you learn from consequences, build trust, and gain greater control over your choices."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do if I blame everyone else for my problems?","x":"Pause and ask what part of the situation is within your control and what action you can take."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is accountability?","x":"Accountability means accepting responsibility for your actions, decisions, and commitments."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is accountability important?","x":"It helps you learn, build trust, correct mistakes, and become more responsible."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I become more responsible?","x":"Keep your commitments, manage your time, admit mistakes, and complete tasks without always being reminded."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is patience?","x":"Patience is the ability to wait and continue acting appropriately without demanding immediate results."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is patience important for students?","x":"Learning and personal growth take time, so patience helps students continue practicing instead of giving up too quickly."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I be patient with my progress?","x":"Track small improvements and remember that progress does not always happen at the same speed."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is self-discipline?","x":"Self-discipline is choosing actions that support your goals even when distractions or temporary feelings pull you in another direction."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I avoid distractions?","x":"Identify your biggest distractions, create a focused study environment, and set specific times for entertainment."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I manage my time?","x":"List your priorities, estimate how long tasks will take, create a schedule, and leave room for rest."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is rest important?","x":"Rest helps your body and mind recover and can support concentration, learning, and healthy functioning."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I balance school and personal life?","x":"Plan your responsibilities, protect time for rest, and avoid trying to do everything at once."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when I feel behind others?","x":"Focus on your own starting point, identify the next useful step, and avoid turning someone else's progress into a judgment about your worth."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if my family or friends do not support my goals?","x":"Listen respectfully, explain why the goal matters, seek constructive support elsewhere, and continue building a realistic plan."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I respond to peer pressure?","x":"Know your values, pause before agreeing, consider the consequences, and confidently say no when something is wrong for you."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a personal value?","x":"A personal value is a principle or quality that guides how you want to live and make decisions."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I know my values?","x":"Knowing your values makes it easier to make consistent decisions when you face pressure or uncertainty."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I make better decisions?","x":"Identify the problem, consider options and consequences, gather useful information, and choose the option that best fits your values and goals."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Should I make decisions when I am extremely angry?","x":"It is usually better to pause and calm down first when possible, because strong emotions can affect judgment."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I solve problems positively?","x":"Define the problem clearly, identify possible solutions, consider consequences, choose a practical step, and evaluate the result."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when a problem has no easy solution?","x":"Accept that some problems are difficult, seek advice, focus on what you can control, and work on the best available option."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is asking for help a strength?","x":"Asking for help shows that you recognize what you need and are willing to learn or seek support."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Who can I ask for help?","x":"Depending on the situation, you can talk to a trusted parent, teacher, mentor, counselor, responsible adult, or other appropriate support person."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I become a better leader?","x":"Lead by example, listen to others, take responsibility, communicate clearly, and use leadership to serve rather than control."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is servant leadership?","x":"Servant leadership means using your position and abilities to support, develop, and help others."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can a student be a leader?","x":"Yes. Leadership can be shown through responsibility, teamwork, kindness, service, and positive example."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What makes a good team member?","x":"A good team member communicates, listens, completes responsibilities, respects others, and helps the group succeed."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I handle disagreement?","x":"Listen to the other person's view, explain your own respectfully, look for common ground, and avoid unnecessary insults."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is respect important?","x":"Respect helps people communicate, cooperate, and live together with dignity."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can I disagree with someone and still respect them?","x":"Yes. You can disagree with an idea or decision while still treating the person respectfully."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I apologize properly?","x":"Acknowledge what you did, take responsibility, express sincere regret, and explain what you will do differently."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is honesty important?","x":"Honesty builds trust and helps people make decisions based on accurate information."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do if I make a promise I cannot keep?","x":"Communicate honestly as soon as possible, explain the situation without making excuses, and take responsibility for finding a solution."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I become a better friend?","x":"Listen, keep reasonable trust, encourage your friend, respect boundaries, and be honest and supportive."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a healthy friendship?","x":"A healthy friendship includes mutual respect, trust, communication, boundaries, and support."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do if a friendship becomes harmful?","x":"Recognize the harmful behavior, set boundaries, seek advice from a trusted person, and create distance if necessary."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why are boundaries important?","x":"Boundaries help protect your time, safety, dignity, and emotional well-being."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is empathy?","x":"Empathy is trying to understand another person's feelings or perspective."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can empathy improve relationships?","x":"It helps you listen better, understand differences, and respond with greater care."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I practice gratitude every day?","x":"Take a few minutes to identify specific people, experiences, opportunities, or things you appreciate."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I celebrate small achievements?","x":"Recognizing small achievements can help you notice progress and maintain motivation."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Does celebrating progress mean I should stop improving?","x":"No. You can appreciate progress while continuing to learn and grow."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is continuous improvement?","x":"Continuous improvement means regularly looking for small ways to make your skills, habits, decisions, or results better."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I learn from my past?","x":"Review what happened, identify lessons, take responsibility for what you can control, and apply those lessons going forward."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Should I live in the past?","x":"No. Your past can teach you, but your present choices are where you can take action."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Should I worry constantly about the future?","x":"Planning for the future is useful, but constant worry can become unhelpful. Focus on realistic preparation and the actions available today."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What can I control?","x":"You can often control your choices, effort, preparation, communication, and how you respond, although you cannot control everything around you."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I focus on what I can control?","x":"It directs your energy toward actions that can actually influence your situation."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a positive self-talk?","x":"Positive self-talk is using realistic, supportive language with yourself instead of automatically attacking or discouraging yourself."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Give me an example of positive self-talk.","x":"Instead of 'I always fail,' try 'This did not go well, but I can learn what went wrong and improve.'"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can positive affirmations help?","x":"They can help when they are realistic and connected to action, such as 'I can learn this through practice.'"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a realistic affirmation?","x":"It is a supportive statement that you can reasonably work toward or believe, rather than an unrealistic promise."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I start changing my mindset today?","x":"Choose one unhelpful thought, question it, replace it with a realistic alternative, and take one positive action."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is one daily mindset challenge?","x":"Choose one useful action you have been avoiding and complete it today."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a daily reflection?","x":"It is a short time when you review what happened, what you learned, what went well, and what you want to improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What three questions can I ask myself each night?","x":"What did I learn today? What did I do well? What will I improve tomorrow?"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I motivate myself in the morning?","x":"Remind yourself of one meaningful goal, choose your priorities, and begin with a small productive action."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when motivation disappears?","x":"Use your routine and discipline to continue with a manageable action rather than waiting for motivation to return."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why do habits matter?","x":"Habits shape repeated behavior, and repeated behavior can influence long-term results."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How long does it take to build a habit?","x":"There is no single number that applies to everyone. Habits develop through repeated practice over time."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do if I break a good habit?","x":"Do not treat one setback as total failure. Restart as soon as practical and identify what caused the interruption."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I become more organized?","x":"Write down responsibilities, prioritize tasks, keep materials in order, and review your plan regularly."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is preparation important?","x":"Preparation can reduce avoidable mistakes, improve confidence, and help you respond better to challenges."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can preparation build confidence?","x":"When you practice and prepare, you gain evidence that you have worked toward being ready."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do before an important exam?","x":"Review key material, practice questions, organize what you need, rest appropriately, and arrive prepared."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stay calm before an exam?","x":"Prepare early, breathe slowly, focus on what you know, and avoid unnecessary last-minute panic."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if I forget something during an exam?","x":"Pause, breathe, move to another question if appropriate, and return to the difficult question later."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How should I respond when someone succeeds before me?","x":"Celebrate their achievement when appropriate and use it as motivation rather than proof that you are behind."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Can someone else's success reduce my chances of success?","x":"Not necessarily. Their success can provide lessons, inspiration, or evidence that a goal may be possible."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I learn from successful people?","x":"You can study their habits, decisions, mistakes, and strategies while remembering that everyone's circumstances are different."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Should I copy another person's life?","x":"No. Learn useful lessons from others, but build a path that fits your own values, abilities, and circumstances."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is purpose?","x":"Purpose is a sense of meaning or direction that helps you understand what matters to you and why you want to act."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I discover my purpose?","x":"Explore your values, interests, strengths, responsibilities, and the problems or causes you care about."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Does everyone find their purpose at the same age?","x":"No. Purpose can develop and change as people learn and gain experience."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I use my talents positively?","x":"Develop your talents through practice and use them in ways that support your goals and benefit others."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if I don't know my talent?","x":"Explore different activities, notice what you enjoy and learn quickly, ask for feedback, and give yourself time to discover your strengths."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a strength?","x":"A strength is a quality, skill, or ability that can help you perform or respond effectively."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a weakness?","x":"A weakness is an area where you currently have less skill, confidence, knowledge, or control and may need development."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Should I be ashamed of my weaknesses?","x":"No. Recognizing weaknesses honestly can help you decide what to learn or improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I turn a weakness into a strength?","x":"Identify the specific gap, practice consistently, seek feedback, and track improvement."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is learning important throughout life?","x":"Learning helps you adapt, solve problems, understand the world, and continue developing your abilities."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is lifelong learning?","x":"Lifelong learning is continuing to gain knowledge and skills throughout your life."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I become a better learner?","x":"Ask questions, practice actively, review regularly, explain ideas in your own words, and learn from feedback."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why should I ask questions?","x":"Questions help you identify what you do not understand and can open the way to deeper learning."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Is it okay not to know something?","x":"Yes. Not knowing something is often the starting point for learning it."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What should I do when someone corrects me?","x":"Listen carefully, decide whether the correction is accurate, thank the person when appropriate, and use the information to improve."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Why is humility important?","x":"Humility allows you to recognize that you can learn from others and that you do not have to know everything."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I stay positive without becoming unrealistic?","x":"Acknowledge problems honestly, consider evidence, prepare for difficulties, and still look for constructive possibilities."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is toxic positivity?","x":"Toxic positivity is forcing positive statements or ignoring real problems instead of acknowledging emotions and addressing the situation."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How should the AI respond to a student who is deeply sad?","x":"It should respond with empathy, avoid judgment, encourage the student to talk to a trusted person, and recommend professional or emergency help when the situation indicates a serious risk."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if a student says they want to hurt themselves?","x":"The AI should take the statement seriously, encourage immediate contact with a trusted adult or emergency/crisis service, and focus on immediate safety rather than motivational advice."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What if a student says they feel worthless?","x":"Acknowledge the pain, remind them that feelings do not determine their worth, encourage support from a trusted person, and ask what is happening without judging them."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can I support someone who is struggling?","x":"Listen without judging, encourage them to seek appropriate support, and stay connected when it is safe and helpful."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"What is a positive community?","x":"A positive community is one where people encourage growth, respect differences, take responsibility, and work together constructively."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"How can students improve their community?","x":"Students can volunteer, help others, respect shared spaces, support learning, and participate responsibly in community activities."},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Daily positive mindset activities","x":"- Name three things you are grateful for today.\n- Write one negative thought and replace it with a realistic, constructive thought.\n- Identify one small goal for today.\n- Write down one thing you learned from a mistake.\n- Encourage another student.\n- Spend 10–15 minutes improving a useful skill.\n- Review one goal and identify your next action.\n- At night, answer: What did I learn? What did I do well? What will I improve tomorrow?"},{"a":"pmo","g":0,"t":"Main student categories","x":"Understand My Mindset; Build Confidence; School and Learning; Goals and Success; Challenges and Failure; Relationships; Leadership; Personal Growth; Change My Thought; Daily Mindset; Quiz Me; I Need Advice."}];

